00:00:03.139 --> 00:00:05.780 align:start position:0%
after more than 30 years of restrictions  farmers in the EU will now be able to  produce as much milk as they want the  dairy sector will no longer be regulated  by the quota scheme which imposed limits  on milk production on the 31st of March  2015 the quota regime comes to an end so  there will be no meal quotas anymore and  though we see the product of this  happening today the decision the  political decision was made in 2003 when  the council decided to bring this regime  to a conclusion in order to allow the  sector to start its path toward a  greater market orientation the shift  towards the markets will allow European  producers to export more and to compete  better with producers from the United  States and Australia they will also  benefit from the growing demand for  dairy produce in the world the quotas  put limits on the amounts of liquid milk  as well as milk powder yogurts and  cheese but why were the restrictions  imposed in the first place milk quotas  were introduced in 1984 and at the  beginning they were meant to last only  five years and to address the problem of  surpluses so to control the milk  production and guarantee stability for  both milk prices and the income of the  farmers but then they were extended and  extended until it was decided that they  should come to an end the milk quotas  were a way of cutting over supply and  putting an end to the so-called butter  mountains and milk lakes the national  quota of every member state was  distributed among individual producers  producers exceeding their individual  quota had to pay fines or surplus levies  farmers have a lot to gain from the  abolition of the scheme but at the same  time there are concerns so what is the  post quota future for the dairy sector  the market outlook for  the dairy sector is positive the world  demand is expected to grow especially in  emerging countries therefore the  European farmers have good possibilities  in the medium-term however in the short  term we have seen a decrease significant  decrease in prices and the introduction  of the Russian ban on EU food products  and this has impacted farmers very hard  and especially in some Member States  therefore the end of quotas is both a  big opportunity but also a challenge as  its last meeting in March the  Agriculture Council called for close  monitoring of the milk sector as  producers adapt to the new situation
 

